=== TOPIC ===
id: c-syntax-basics
title: C Syntax & Compilation Process
roadmapId: c
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Beginner
estimatedMinutes: 45
summary: Understand the preprocessing, compilation, assembly, and linking phases in C, along with main() and standard headers.
explanation: C is a statically-typed compiled language. Source files (.c) undergo four phases: Preprocessing (macro expansion, include file substitution via cpp), Compilation (syntactic and semantic translation to assembly via cc1), Assembly (converting assembly to machine object code .o via as), and Linking (resolving symbol tables and linking runtime libraries like libc via ld).
codeExample: #include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(int argc, char *argv[]) {\n    printf("Hello, PathForge! Arguments received: %d\n", argc);\n    return 0;\n}
objective: Explain the four stages of GCC toolchain: Preprocessor, Compiler, Assembler, Linker
objective: Write standard-compliant main() signatures with argc and argv
objective: Use headers (#include <stdio.h>) and define macros
pitfall: Omitting return type 'int' for main()
pitfall: Assuming #include injects compiled binaries rather than raw text headers
pitfall: Ignoring compiler warnings (-Wall -Wextra)
prerequisites: 
resource: GCC Compilation Process (Stanford CS107)|https://web.stanford.edu/class/cs107/|article
resource: C Primer Plus: Structure of a C Program|https://en.cppreference.com/w/c|documentation

=== TOPIC ===
id: c-variables
title: C Variables, Types & Memory Footprint
roadmapId: c
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Beginner
estimatedMinutes: 50
summary: Fundamental primitive types (char, int, float, double), sign qualifiers, sizeof operator, and memory representations.
explanation: In C, variables are typed memory locations whose byte sizes depend on machine architecture and ABI standards. Primitive types map directly to hardware registers and word sizes. The sizeof operator computes compile-time byte width. Fixed-width integer types from <stdint.h> (int32_t, uint64_t) ensure portability across distinct microarchitectures.
codeExample: #include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdint.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int32_t count = 42;\n    uint8_t flags = 0xFF;\n    printf("Size of int32: %zu bytes, uint8: %zu bytes\n", sizeof(count), sizeof(flags));\n    return 0;\n}
objective: Distinguish signed and unsigned representation and overflow behavior
objective: Inspect memory footprints using sizeof() operator
objective: Apply portable fixed-width integers using <stdint.h>
pitfall: Assuming int is always 32 bits across 16-bit embedded or 64-bit platforms
pitfall: Signed integer overflow invokes Undefined Behavior (UB)
pitfall: Truncation during implicit type casting between signed and unsigned
prerequisites: c-syntax-basics
resource: SEI CERT C Coding Standard: Integers|https://wiki.sei.cmu.edu/confluence/display/c|reference
resource: C Data Types & Storage Classes|https://en.cppreference.com/w/c/language/types|documentation

=== TOPIC ===
id: c-control-flow
title: C Control Flow & Branching
roadmapId: c
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Beginner
estimatedMinutes: 40
summary: Direct execution paths using if/else, switch jump tables, while, do-while, and for loops.
explanation: Control flow statements govern execution sequencing. In C, any non-zero integer evaluates to logical true, while 0 evaluates to false. The switch statement compiles into efficient jump tables or binary search branches when evaluating integral constants.
codeExample: #include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {\n        if (i % 2 == 0) {\n            printf("%d is even\n", i);\n        } else {\n            printf("%d is odd\n", i);\n        }\n    }\n    return 0;\n}
objective: Construct nested conditionals and multi-way switch statements
objective: Select optimal iteration constructs between while, do-while, and for
objective: Utilize break and continue statements without spaghetti control flow
pitfall: Using assignment = instead of equality == inside if conditions
pitfall: Missing break statements in switch cases leading to unintended fall-through
pitfall: Off-by-one errors in loop boundaries
prerequisites: c-variables
resource: C Control Statements & Branch Prediction|https://en.cppreference.com/w/c/language/if|documentation

=== TOPIC ===
id: c-functions
title: C Functions & Call Stack Architecture
roadmapId: c
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Beginner
estimatedMinutes: 60
summary: Function prototypes, call-by-value semantics, stack frames, return values, and recursion.
explanation: Functions encapsulate reusable units of computation. C enforces pass-by-value: function arguments are pushed onto the thread call stack as local frame copies. To allow mutation of caller state, memory addresses (pointers) must be passed explicitly.
codeExample: #include <stdio.h>\n\nlong factorial(int n) {\n    if (n <= 1) return 1;\n    return n * factorial(n - 1);\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    printf("5! = %ld\n", factorial(5));\n    return 0;\n}
objective: Declare clean header prototypes and implementation definitions
objective: Explain stack frame allocation and stack pointer (RSP/ESP) movement
objective: Design recursive functions with explicit terminating base cases
pitfall: Returning a pointer to a local stack-allocated variable
pitfall: Infinite recursion leading to stack overflow crash
pitfall: Missing forward prototypes causing compiler implicit declaration warnings
prerequisites: c-control-flow
resource: Computer Systems: A Programmer's Perspective (Stack Architecture)|https://csapp.cs.cmu.edu/|book

=== TOPIC ===
id: c-pointers-memory
title: C Pointers, Addresses & Pointer Arithmetic
roadmapId: c
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 90
summary: Direct memory addressing, dereferencing (*), address-of (&), pointer arithmetic, and void pointers.
explanation: A pointer is a variable holding the virtual memory address of another entity. Pointer arithmetic scales automatically by the byte size of the underlying type (e.g. ptr + 1 advances sizeof(*ptr) bytes). Pointers enable in-place mutation, array indexing, and building dynamic data structures.
codeExample: #include <stdio.h>\n\nvoid swap(int *a, int *b) {\n    int temp = *a;\n    *a = *b;\n    *b = temp;\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    int x = 10, y = 20;\n    swap(&x, &y);\n    printf("x = %d, y = %d\n", x, y);\n    return 0;\n}
objective: Master address-of (&) and dereference (*) operators
objective: Perform safe pointer arithmetic over contiguous memory arrays
objective: Inspect and manipulate raw memory buffers with generic void* pointers
pitfall: Dereferencing NULL or uninitialized (dangling) pointers causing segmentation faults
pitfall: Pointer arithmetic out-of-bounds corrupting neighboring memory
pitfall: Confusing pointer-to-const with const-pointer
prerequisites: c-functions
resource: Understanding Pointers in C - Deep Dive|https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pointer_(computer_programming)|reference

=== TOPIC ===
id: c-structs
title: C Structs, Unions & Memory Alignment
roadmapId: c
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 60
summary: Heterogeneous data structures, typedef alias, memory padding, byte alignment, and bitfields.
explanation: Structs package related fields of differing data types into a unified memory block. Hardware alignment rules dictate that data types must align to boundaries divisible by their size (e.g., 4-byte ints align to 4-byte boundaries), causing the compiler to inject padding bytes. Structs form the foundation for nodes in linked lists, trees, and graphs.
codeExample: #include <stdio.h>\n\ntypedef struct {\n    int id;\n    double score;\n} Student;\n\nvoid printStudent(const Student *s) {\n    printf("Student #%d: Score %.1f\n", s->id, s->score);\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    Student s = { .id = 101, .score = 94.5 };\n    printStudent(&s);\n    return 0;\n}
objective: Define structured types with typedef for clean API design
objective: Predict structure memory layout and compiler padding bytes
objective: Pass structs efficiently via const pointers to prevent full-copy overhead
pitfall: Direct struct comparison with memcmp() failing due to garbage padding bytes
pitfall: Copying large structs by value on the stack instead of passing pointers
pitfall: Forgetting arrow operator (->) for dereferencing struct pointer members
prerequisites: c-pointers-memory
resource: The Lost Art of Structure Packing|http://www.catb.org/esr/structure-packing/|article

=== TOPIC ===
id: c-dynamic-allocation
title: Dynamic Heap Allocation: malloc, calloc, realloc & free
roadmapId: c
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Advanced
estimatedMinutes: 80
summary: Managing heap memory lifecycles, detecting memory leaks, and avoiding double-free vulnerabilities.
explanation: Heap memory allows dynamic sizing at runtime via malloc (uninitialized), calloc (zero-initialized), and realloc (in-place resize or relocation). Heap allocations persist across function calls until explicitly released with free(). Every allocated byte must be accounted for to prevent memory leaks and fragmentation.
codeExample: #include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int *arr = malloc(5 * sizeof(int));\n    if (!arr) return 1;\n    for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) arr[i] = (i + 1) * 10;\n    for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) printf("%d ", arr[i]);\n    printf("\n");\n    free(arr);\n    arr = NULL;\n    return 0;\n}
objective: Allocate and release dynamic blocks with malloc(), calloc(), and free()
objective: Resize dynamic buffers safely with realloc() preserving existing data
objective: Enforce pointer nullification post-free to prevent use-after-free bugs
pitfall: Failing to check if malloc/calloc returned NULL on memory exhaustion
pitfall: Double freeing the same heap block causing heap corruption
pitfall: Memory leaks when assigning a new pointer over un-freed allocated memory
prerequisites: c-structs
resource: Valgrind Memory Debugger Handbook|https://valgrind.org/docs/manual/quick-start.html|tool

=== TOPIC ===
id: c-file-io
title: C File I/O & Stream Handling
roadmapId: c
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 55
summary: Standard I/O streams, binary and text file manipulation with fopen, fread, fwrite, fgets, and fclose.
explanation: C abstracts file access through buffered file streams managed by the FILE handle. Operations differentiate between formatted text streams (fprintf, fscanf, fgets) and raw binary streams (fread, fwrite). Proper error checking with ferror() and stream flushing with fflush() are critical for data persistence.
codeExample: #include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    FILE *fp = fopen("log.txt", "w+");\n    if (!fp) { perror("Failed to open file"); return 1; }\n    fprintf(fp, "PathForge C Runtime: OK\n");\n    rewind(fp);\n    char line[128];\n    if (fgets(line, sizeof(line), fp)) {\n        printf("Read: %s", line);\n    }\n    fclose(fp);\n    return 0;\n}
objective: Open, read, write, and close files with robust error handling
objective: Stream formatted text and serialize binary records with fread/fwrite
objective: Inspect EOF indicators and seek stream offsets with fseek/ftell
pitfall: Testing for EOF before reading, resulting in duplicate processing of the last line
pitfall: Leaving open file handles causing OS descriptor exhaustion leaks
pitfall: Buffer overflow when reading untrusted input using gets() instead of fgets()
prerequisites: c-dynamic-allocation
resource: GNU C Library: Input/Output on Streams|https://www.gnu.org/software/libc/manual/html_node/I_002fO-on-Streams.html|documentation

=== TOPIC ===
id: java-syntax-basics
title: Java Syntax, JVM Architecture & Bytecode
roadmapId: java
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Beginner
estimatedMinutes: 45
summary: Class structures, public static void main, JVM compilation, bytecode verification, and JIT compilation.
explanation: Java programs compile to intermediate platform-agnostic bytecode (.class) executed by the Java Virtual Machine (JVM). The JVM leverages Just-In-Time (JIT) compilation (e.g. HotSpot C1/C2) to translate hot bytecode loops into native machine instructions at runtime.
codeExample: public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println("Java JVM initialized successfully. Args: " + args.length);\n    }\n}
objective: Explain JVM, JRE, and JDK distinctions and the bytecode execution model
objective: Construct proper Java class declarations and entry-point main methods
objective: Compile and execute using javac and java CLI tools
pitfall: Mismatch between public class name and .java filename
pitfall: Incorrect main method signature preventing runtime entry point discovery
pitfall: Assuming Java bytecode is strictly interpreted without JIT optimization
prerequisites: 
resource: The Java Virtual Machine Specification (Oracle)|https://docs.oracle.com/javase/specs/jvms/se17/html/|documentation

=== TOPIC ===
id: java-variables
title: Java Primitive Types, Wrappers & Object References
roadmapId: java
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Beginner
estimatedMinutes: 45
summary: 8 primitives (byte, short, int, long, float, double, char, boolean), wrapper classes, autoboxing, and object references.
explanation: Java maintains a clear division between 8 value-based primitive types stored directly in stack frames or object fields, and reference types pointing to heap-allocated objects. Wrapper classes (Integer, Double) bridge primitives into object collections through compiler autoboxing.
codeExample: public class VariablesDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int primitive = 100;\n        Integer wrapper = primitive; // Autoboxing\n        System.out.println("Equality: " + (primitive == wrapper));\n    }\n}
objective: Categorize primitive data types by bit width and default values
objective: Demonstrate autoboxing and unboxing behaviors and performance costs
objective: Differentiate value equality (==) from object equivalence (.equals())
pitfall: Using == instead of .equals() on String and wrapper instances
pitfall: NullPointerException when unboxing a null wrapper object
pitfall: Memory overhead from excessive autoboxing in performance-critical loops
prerequisites: java-syntax-basics
resource: Oracle Java Tutorials: Primitive Data Types|https://docs.oracle.com/javase/tutorial/java/nutsandbolts/datatypes.html|documentation

=== TOPIC ===
id: java-control-flow
title: Java Flow Control & Enhanced Switch
roadmapId: java
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Beginner
estimatedMinutes: 40
summary: Conditionals, traditional and enhanced switch expressions, while/for loops, and for-each iteration.
explanation: Java provides structured branching and iteration constructs. Modern Java supports arrow syntax switch expressions that return values directly, yield keywords, and exhaustive pattern matching without risk of fall-through.
codeExample: public class FlowControl {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        String day = "WED";\n        int dayNum = switch (day) {\n            case "MON" -> 1;\n            case "WED" -> 3;\n            case "FRI" -> 5;\n            default -> 0;\n        };\n        System.out.println("Day number: " + dayNum);\n    }\n}
objective: Formulate conditionals and ternary operators
objective: Leverage modern switch expressions with yield and arrow syntax
objective: Iterate over arrays and collections using enhanced for-each loops
pitfall: Modifying a collection during enhanced for-each iteration (ConcurrentModificationException)
pitfall: Comparing strings with == in switch statements on older Java releases
prerequisites: java-variables
resource: JEP 361: Switch Expressions (Java SE 14+)|https://openjdk.org/jeps/361|article

=== TOPIC ===
id: java-methods
title: Java Methods, Pass-By-Value & Overloading
roadmapId: java
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Beginner
estimatedMinutes: 50
summary: Method signatures, pass-by-value of object references, method overloading, and varargs.
explanation: All Java methods belong to a class. Java is strictly pass-by-value: primitive parameters receive copies of values, while object parameters receive copies of reference addresses. Method overloading permits multiple methods with identical names but differing argument signatures.
codeExample: public class MethodsDemo {\n    public static int sum(int... numbers) {\n        int total = 0;\n        for (int n : numbers) total += n;\n        return total;\n    }\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println("Sum: " + sum(10, 20, 30, 40));\n    }\n}
objective: Author static and instance methods with precise access modifiers
objective: Demonstrate pass-by-value semantics with object mutations vs reassignment
objective: Implement method overloading and variable-length argument lists (varargs)
pitfall: Assuming Java is pass-by-reference when mutating object properties inside methods
pitfall: Ambiguous method overloading signatures resulting in compile errors
prerequisites: java-control-flow
resource: Java Language Specification: Method Declarations|https://docs.oracle.com/javase/specs/jls/se17/html/jls-8.html#jls-8.4|documentation

=== TOPIC ===
id: java-classes-oop
title: Java OOP: Encapsulation, Inheritance & Polymorphism
roadmapId: java
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 75
summary: Classes, constructors, encapsulation with access modifiers, interface contracts, and dynamic dispatch.
explanation: Object-Oriented Programming models domains through encapsulated objects. Inheritance (extends) creates specialization hierarchies, while interfaces (implements) define polymorphic capability contracts. Dynamic method dispatch enables polymorphic behavior resolved at runtime.
codeExample: interface Shape {\n    double area();\n}\nclass Circle implements Shape {\n    private final double radius;\n    public Circle(double r) { this.radius = r; }\n    @Override public double area() { return Math.PI * radius * radius; }\n}\npublic class OOPDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Shape s = new Circle(5.0);\n        System.out.println("Circle area: " + s.area());\n    }\n}
objective: Encapsulate state using private fields and public accessor methods
objective: Inherit behavior with super and override annotations
objective: Design clean interfaces to decouple implementations from caller code
pitfall: Overusing deep inheritance trees instead of composition
pitfall: Forgetting @Override annotation causing accidental method overloading instead of overriding
pitfall: Leaking mutable internal representations through getters
prerequisites: java-methods
resource: Effective Java by Joshua Bloch (OOP Guidelines)|https://www.pearson.com/|book

=== TOPIC ===
id: java-collections-framework
title: Java Collections Framework & Generics
roadmapId: java
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 80
summary: List (ArrayList, LinkedList), Set (HashSet, TreeSet), Map (HashMap, TreeMap), and generics type safety.
explanation: The Java Collections Framework provides standardized data structures in java.util. Collections leverage generics (<T>) to provide compile-time type safety with type erasure. Choosing the right implementation (e.g. ArrayList O(1) random access vs LinkedList O(1) edge inserts) determines performance.
codeExample: import java.util.*;\npublic class CollectionsDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Map<String, Integer> scores = new HashMap<>();\n        scores.put("Alice", 95);\n        scores.put("Bob", 88);\n        scores.forEach((name, score) -> System.out.println(name + ": " + score));\n    }\n}
objective: Select appropriate collection interfaces (List, Set, Queue, Map)
objective: Compare HashMap vs TreeMap time complexity and ordering guarantees
objective: Implement generic classes and methods with bounded type wildcards
pitfall: Failing to override hashCode() when overriding equals() on Map keys
pitfall: Choosing LinkedList assuming faster general inserts without considering cache locality
pitfall: Unchecked type casts bypassing compile-time generic safety
prerequisites: java-classes-oop
resource: Java Collections Framework Guide|https://docs.oracle.com/javase/8/docs/technotes/guides/collections/overview.html|documentation

=== TOPIC ===
id: java-exception-handling
title: Java Exception Hierarchy & Robust Error Recovery
roadmapId: java
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 50
summary: Throwable, Checked vs Unchecked exceptions, try-catch-finally, try-with-resources, and custom exceptions.
explanation: Java separates exceptional conditions into Errors (unrecoverable system faults), Checked Exceptions (subclasses of Exception requiring explicit throws or catch handling), and Unchecked Exceptions (subclasses of RuntimeException). Try-with-resources guarantees automatic closure of AutoCloseable resources.
codeExample: import java.io.*;\npublic class ExceptionDemo {\n    public static void readFile(String path) throws IOException {\n        try (BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(path))) {\n            System.out.println("Header: " + br.readLine());\n        }\n    }\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        try { readFile("missing.txt"); }\n        catch (IOException e) { System.err.println("Handled: " + e.getMessage()); }\n    }\n}
objective: Distinguish when to use checked exceptions vs unchecked runtime exceptions
objective: Manage resource leaks cleanly with try-with-resources blocks
objective: Craft informative domain-specific custom exception classes
pitfall: Catching generic Exception or Throwable and swallowing errors silently
pitfall: Resource leaks by manually closing streams in catch instead of try-with-resources
pitfall: Using exceptions for ordinary control flow logic
prerequisites: java-collections-framework
resource: Oracle Exceptions Tutorial|https://docs.oracle.com/javase/tutorial/essential/exceptions/|documentation

=== TOPIC ===
id: java-multithreading
title: Java Concurrency & Thread Coordination
roadmapId: java
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Advanced
estimatedMinutes: 90
summary: Threads, Runnable, synchronized blocks, volatile visibility, ExecutorService, and modern Virtual Threads.
explanation: Java provides native multithreading mapped to OS threads. Synchronization guarantees mutual exclusion and memory visibility across CPU caches. The java.util.concurrent package provides thread pools, locks, and atomic primitives. Java 21 introduces lightweight Virtual Threads for high-throughput I/O.
codeExample: import java.util.concurrent.*;\npublic class ThreadDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {\n        ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(2);\n        executor.submit(() -> System.out.println("Worker running: " + Thread.currentThread().getName()));\n        executor.shutdown();\n        executor.awaitTermination(1, TimeUnit.SECONDS);\n    }\n}
objective: Spawn tasks using Thread and modern ExecutorService pools
objective: Prevent race conditions and deadlocks using synchronized and ReentrantLock
objective: Explain memory visibility and ordering guarantees established by volatile
pitfall: Deadlock caused by acquiring locks in inconsistent order
pitfall: Relying on volatile for compound non-atomic operations (e.g. counter++)
pitfall: Failing to shut down ExecutorService causing JVM to hang on exit
prerequisites: java-exception-handling
resource: Java Concurrency in Practice by Brian Goetz|https://jcip.net/|book

=== TOPIC ===
id: python-syntax-basics
title: Python Syntax, REPL & Dynamic Execution
roadmapId: python
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Beginner
estimatedMinutes: 35
summary: Indentation-based syntax, interactive REPL, comments, docstrings, and Python execution model.
explanation: Python is an interpreted, high-level, dynamically typed language emphasizing human readability. Indentation defines statement blocks. Source code is compiled to bytecode (.pyc) and executed on the CPython virtual machine.
codeExample: # PathForge Python Introduction\ndef greet(target: str) -> str:\n    """Return an enthusiastic welcome message."""\n    return f"Welcome to {target}!"\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    print(greet("PathForge"))
objective: Structure programs using consistent whitespace indentation (PEP 8)
objective: Execute scripts and experiment interactively in the REPL
objective: Document modules and functions using structured docstrings
pitfall: Mixing tabs and spaces resulting in TabError
pitfall: Misunderstanding that Python code is compiled to bytecode before execution
pitfall: Omitting the if __name__ == '__main__' guard in executable scripts
prerequisites: 
resource: PEP 8: Style Guide for Python Code|https://peps.python.org/pep-0008/|reference

=== TOPIC ===
id: python-variables
title: Python Variables, Dynamic Typing & Object Identity
roadmapId: python
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Beginner
estimatedMinutes: 40
summary: Variables as pointer references, dynamic typing, mutable vs immutable types, and id() introspection.
explanation: In Python, variables are names bound to objects in memory; they carry no type information themselves. Built-in types are categorized into immutable (int, float, str, tuple) and mutable (list, dict, set). Variable assignment binds a reference rather than copying object data.
codeExample: a = [1, 2, 3]\nb = a # b references the identical list object\nb.append(4)\nprint(f"a: {a}, identical identity: {a is b}")\n\nx = 500\ny = 500\nprint(f"Value equal: {x == y}, Identity equal: {x is y}")
objective: Distinguish value equality (==) from reference identity (is)
objective: Differentiate mutable and immutable data type behaviors
objective: Inspect memory addresses and reference counts using id() and sys
pitfall: Using mutable default arguments (e.g. def fn(arg=[])) causing state leakage
pitfall: Assuming variable assignment copies compound structures like lists
pitfall: Overusing is instead of == when comparing numeric values
prerequisites: python-syntax-basics
resource: Fluent Python: Objects and Mutability|https://www.oreilly.com/library/view/fluent-python-2nd/9781492056348/|book

=== TOPIC ===
id: python-control-flow
title: Python Control Flow, Pattern Matching & Comprehensions
roadmapId: python
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Beginner
estimatedMinutes: 45
summary: if-elif-else branching, while and for loops, enumerate, zip, and structural pattern matching (match-case).
explanation: Python provides idiomatic iteration over iterables using for...in. Modern Python includes structural pattern matching (match...case) allowing structural destructuring of sequences and mappings. Comprehensions provide concise, functional syntax for building sequences.
codeExample: status_code = 404\nmessage = match status_code:\n    case 200: "OK"\n    case 404: "Not Found"\n    case _: "Unhandled Code"\nprint(f"Status {status_code}: {message}")\n\nsquares = [x**2 for x in range(6) if x % 2 == 0]\nprint(f"Even squares: {squares}")
objective: Iterate idiomatically using range(), enumerate(), and zip()
objective: Apply structural pattern matching to parse complex nested inputs
objective: Write expressive list, set, and dictionary comprehensions
pitfall: Modifying a list while iterating over it
pitfall: Writing deeply nested, unreadable list comprehensions instead of plain loops
pitfall: Assuming range() generates an in-memory list rather than a lazy generator
prerequisites: python-variables
resource: PEP 636: Structural Pattern Matching Tutorial|https://peps.python.org/pep-0636/|article

=== TOPIC ===
id: python-functions
title: Python Functions, Scope & First-Class Citizens
roadmapId: python
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Beginner
estimatedMinutes: 55
summary: LEGB scope resolution, *args, **kwargs, lambda expressions, closures, and decorators.
explanation: Functions in Python are first-class objects: they can be assigned to variables, passed as arguments, and returned from other functions. Scope resolution follows the LEGB rule (Local, Enclosing, Global, Built-in). Closures and decorators wrap and extend function behaviors dynamically.
codeExample: def timer_decorator(func):\n    def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):\n        print(f"Calling {func.__name__}...")\n        return func(*args, **kwargs)\n    return wrapper\n\n@timer_decorator\ndef compute(n: int) -> int:\n    return sum(range(n))\n\nprint("Result:", compute(1000))
objective: Construct functions accepting variable keyword and positional arguments
objective: Apply LEGB scoping rules and global/nonlocal declarations
objective: Build reusable higher-order function decorators
pitfall: Late binding in closures within loops leading to surprising identical captured values
pitfall: Unintended modification of globals without explicit global keyword
pitfall: Overcomplicating simple logic with unnecessary lambda expressions
prerequisites: python-control-flow
resource: Python Documentation: Defining Functions|https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/controlflow.html#defining-functions|documentation

=== TOPIC ===
id: python-data-structures
title: Python Built-in Structures: Lists, Tuples, Dictionaries & Sets
roadmapId: python
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 60
summary: Dynamic array lists, immutable tuples, hash table dictionaries and sets, and time complexities.
explanation: Python provides optimized built-in collections. Lists are dynamically-resized pointer arrays (O(1) append/pop right, O(N) insert left). Tuples are lightweight immutable sequences. Dictionaries and sets are separate-chaining / open-addressing hash tables providing average O(1) key lookups.
codeExample: catalog = {"c": 10, "java": 20, "python": 30}\n# Safe lookup with default\nprint("Rust count:", catalog.get("rust", 0))\n\nskills_a = {"algorithms", "git", "docker"}\nskills_b = {"docker", "k8s", "terraform"}\nprint("Common skills:", skills_a & skills_b)
objective: Select optimal data structures based on Big-O access and insertion complexities
objective: Leverage dictionary hashing, get() defaults, and views (.items(), .keys())
objective: Apply set theory operations (union, intersection, difference) for high-speed deduplication
pitfall: Using list membership check (item in my_list) O(N) when set membership O(1) is needed
pitfall: KeyError when indexing missing dictionary keys directly instead of using .get()
pitfall: Attempting to use mutable objects (lists) as dictionary keys or set members
prerequisites: python-functions
resource: Python TimeComplexity Wiki|https://wiki.python.org/moin/TimeComplexity|reference

=== TOPIC ===
id: python-modules-packages
title: Python Modules, Packages & Import Machinery
roadmapId: python
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 45
summary: Module creation, __init__.py package structuring, absolute vs relative imports, and sys.path.
explanation: Any Python file is a module. Directories containing __init__.py files define packages. The import statement searches sys.path to load modules, caching them into sys.modules to prevent redundant execution.
codeExample: # Inside mypackage/__init__.py\nfrom .math_utils import add, multiply\n__all__ = ['add', 'multiply']\n\n# In main.py\n# from mypackage import add\nprint("Package initialized cleanly")
objective: Structure modular multi-file project packages
objective: Differentiate absolute imports from explicit relative imports
objective: Troubleshoot circular import dependencies and sys.path issues
pitfall: Circular imports causing AttributeError on partially initialized modules
pitfall: Naming custom files after standard library modules (e.g. math.py or json.py)
pitfall: Using wildcard imports (from module import *) polluting the namespace
prerequisites: python-data-structures
resource: Python Packaging User Guide|https://packaging.python.org/|documentation

=== TOPIC ===
id: python-file-io-json
title: Python File I/O, Context Managers & JSON Serialization
roadmapId: python
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 50
summary: Reading and writing files with the with statement, encoding, and json encoding/decoding.
explanation: The with statement defines a context manager ensuring file descriptors are automatically closed upon block exit, even when exceptions arise. Python's built-in json module seamlessly converts native dictionaries, lists, strings, and numbers into JSON strings and files.
codeExample: import json\n\ndata = {"name": "PathForge", "version": 1.0, "active": True}\nwith open("config.json", "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:\n    json.dump(data, f, indent=2)\n\nwith open("config.json", "r", encoding="utf-8") as f:\n    loaded = json.load(f)\n    print("Loaded JSON:", loaded["name"])
objective: Open and manipulate text files safely with context managers
objective: Serialize and deserialize nested Python objects using json.dumps and json.loads
objective: Handle file I/O exceptions (FileNotFoundError, PermissionError)
pitfall: Omitting explicit encoding='utf-8' leading to platform-dependent decoding errors
pitfall: Attempting to JSON-serialize custom class instances without custom encoders
pitfall: Forgetting that json keys must be strings in standard JSON format
prerequisites: python-modules-packages
resource: Python json Module Documentation|https://docs.python.org/3/library/json.html|documentation

=== TOPIC ===
id: python-virtual-environments
title: Python Virtual Environments & Dependency Isolation
roadmapId: python
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 40
summary: Creating isolated Python runtimes using venv, dependency locking with pip and requirements.txt.
explanation: Virtual environments isolate project-specific third-party packages, preventing version collisions with the system Python or other projects. Tools like venv, pip-tools, and modern package managers (uv, poetry) generate lockfiles guaranteeing reproducible builds across development and production.
codeExample: # Terminal Commands:\n# python -m venv .venv\n# source .venv/bin/activate  (or .venv\Scripts\activate on Windows)\n# pip install requests\n# pip freeze > requirements.txt\nprint("Virtual environment lifecycle mastered")
objective: Generate and activate virtual environments using python -m venv
objective: Install, manage, and pin packages using pip and requirements.txt
objective: Audit project dependencies for vulnerabilities and conflicts
pitfall: Committing the .venv folder into Git version control
pitfall: Installing packages globally without activating the project environment
pitfall: Using unpinned dependency versions leading to unexpected breaking upgrades
prerequisites: python-file-io-json
resource: Python Virtual Environments Guide|https://docs.python.org/3/library/venv.html|documentation

=== TOPIC ===
id: aiml-math-linear-algebra
title: Linear Algebra & Multivariable Calculus for ML
roadmapId: ai-ml-engineer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 90
summary: Vectors, matrices, dot products, eigenvalues, partial derivatives, and gradient descent.
explanation: Machine learning represents data as high-dimensional vectors and model weights as matrices. Matrix multiplications form the computational core of neural network forward passes. Multivariable calculus and the chain rule drive backward propagation to calculate parameter gradients for optimization.
codeExample: # Conceptual Vector Dot Product in Python\nv1 = [1.0, 2.0, 3.0]\nv2 = [4.0, 5.0, 6.0]\ndot_product = sum(a * b for a, b in zip(v1, v2))\nprint(f"Dot product: {dot_product}") # 1*4 + 2*5 + 3*6 = 32
objective: Compute vector dot products, matrix multiplications, and Euclidean norms
objective: Explain eigenvalues, eigenvectors, and dimensionality reduction via PCA
objective: Derive gradients of loss functions with respect to model parameters
pitfall: Confusing element-wise matrix multiplication (Hadamard product) with dot product
pitfall: Dimension mismatch errors in matrix multiplications (A_mxk * B_kxn = C_mxn)
pitfall: Vanishing or exploding gradients during deep backpropagation
prerequisites: python-functions
resource: Essence of Linear Algebra by 3Blue1Brown|https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PLZHQObOWTQDPD3MizzM2xVFitgF8hE_ab|course
resource: Mathematics for Machine Learning (Deisenroth et al.)|https://mml-book.github.io/|book

