id: aiml-deep-learning-neural-nets
title: Deep Learning: Neural Architectures with PyTorch
roadmapId: ai-ml-engineer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Advanced
estimatedMinutes: 100
summary: Perceptrons, activation functions (ReLU, GELU), PyTorch tensors, autograd, and training loops.
explanation: Deep learning models hierarchical representations through multilayer neural networks. PyTorch provides dynamic computational graphs and automatic differentiation (autograd). Training loops iterate epochs: computing forward activations, loss evaluation, optimizer.zero_grad(), loss.backward(), and optimizer.step().
codeExample: import torch\nimport torch.nn as nn\n\nclass Net(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.fc = nn.Linear(10, 1)\n    def forward(self, x):\n        return self.fc(x)\n\nmodel = Net()\nx = torch.randn(2, 10)\nprint("Forward output:", model(x).shape)
objective: Implement custom neural network modules subclassing torch.nn.Module
objective: Construct custom training and validation loops with Adam optimizer and loss functions
objective: Explain modern architectural components: Convolution, Attention, and Residual connections
pitfall: Forgetting optimizer.zero_grad() leading to gradient accumulation across batches
pitfall: Leaving the model in train() mode during validation rather than eval()
pitfall: Mismatch in tensor device placement (CPU vs CUDA/GPU)
prerequisites: aiml-supervised-learning
resource: Deep Learning with PyTorch: A 60 Minute Blitz|https://pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/deep_learning_60min_blitz.html|documentation

