=== TOPIC ===
id: backend-internet-protocols
title: Internet Protocols: TCP/IP, DNS, TLS & HTTP/1-3
roadmapId: backend-developer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Beginner
estimatedMinutes: 60
summary: OSI 7-layer model, TCP three-way handshake, UDP, DNS resolution, TLS encryption, and HTTP lifecycles.
explanation: Backend systems operate on top of internet transport layers. TCP provides reliable, ordered, stream-oriented delivery via three-way handshakes (SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK) and flow control. DNS translates human hostnames into IP addresses. TLS 1.3 secures transport channels with asymmetric handshakes and symmetric AES encryption.
codeExample: # Demonstrating HTTP Request over Socket\nimport socket\ns = socket.socket(socket.AF_INET, socket.SOCK_STREAM)\ns.connect(("example.com", 80))\ns.sendall(b"GET / HTTP/1.1\r\nHost: example.com\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n")\nresponse = s.recv(512)\nprint(response.decode('utf-8', errors='ignore')[:100])\ns.close()
objective: Trace packet lifecycles across the OSI and TCP/IP stack
objective: Explain TCP vs UDP trade-offs (reliability vs latency)
objective: Describe the TLS 1.3 handshake and certificate chain validation
pitfall: Ignoring connection keep-alive causing excessive TCP handshake latency
pitfall: Treating TCP as a packet protocol rather than a continuous byte stream
pitfall: Failing to renew or properly validate TLS certificates
prerequisites: 
resource: High Performance Browser Networking by Ilya Grigorik|https://hpbn.co/|book

=== TOPIC ===
id: backend-restful-api-design
title: RESTful API Architecture, Status Codes & OpenAPI
roadmapId: backend-developer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Beginner
estimatedMinutes: 55
summary: Resource-oriented URL design, HTTP verbs (GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE), status codes, and OpenAPI contracts.
explanation: REST (Representational State Transfer) structures APIs around resources identified by URIs. Standard HTTP methods express actions (GET for safe reads, POST for creation, PUT for idempotent replacement, PATCH for partial updates, DELETE for removal). Standard status codes (2xx, 3xx, 4xx, 5xx) communicate outcomes cleanly.
codeExample: GET /api/v1/topics/c-variables HTTP/1.1\nHost: localhost:8000\nAccept: application/json\n\nHTTP/1.1 200 OK\nContent-Type: application/json\n\n{"id":"c-variables","title":"C Variables"}
objective: Design clean, hierarchical, pluralized resource URIs (e.g. /api/v1/users/42/orders)
objective: Enforce HTTP method idempotency and proper status code semantics
objective: Author declarative OpenAPI / Swagger 3.0 API contracts
pitfall: Using verbs in URI endpoints (e.g. /api/getUser rather than /api/users/1)
pitfall: Returning 200 OK with an error message payload inside the body
pitfall: Making non-idempotent GET endpoints that mutate server state
prerequisites: backend-internet-protocols
resource: RESTful Web Services Guidelines|https://restfulapi.net/|article
resource: OpenAPI Specification 3.0|https://spec.openapis.org/oas/latest.html|documentation

