id: backend-relational-databases-sql
title: Relational Databases: SQL, Indexes & ACID Transactions
roadmapId: backend-developer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 85
summary: Schema design, joins, B-tree indexes, execution plans (EXPLAIN), transactions, and isolation levels.
explanation: Relational databases (PostgreSQL, MySQL, SQLite) ensure data integrity via ACID (Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability) guarantees. Indexes (predominantly B-Trees) enable O(log N) record lookups. Query optimizers evaluate cost-based plans.
codeExample: CREATE TABLE users (\n    id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,\n    email VARCHAR(255) UNIQUE NOT NULL,\n    created_at TIMESTAMP DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP\n);\n\nCREATE INDEX idx_users_email ON users(email);\n\nBEGIN;\nUPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = 1;\nUPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 100 WHERE id = 2;\nCOMMIT;
objective: Design normalized relational schemas (3NF) with foreign key constraints
objective: Optimize query performance using B-Tree and compound indexes
objective: Analyze query plans using EXPLAIN ANALYZE to identify sequential table scans
pitfall: N+1 query anti-pattern in Object-Relational Mappers (ORMs)
pitfall: Missing indexes on foreign keys and frequently filtered query columns
pitfall: Unindexed full-table locks during heavy transactional mutations
prerequisites: backend-dev-subroadmap
resource: Use The Index, Luke! A Guide to Database Performance|https://use-the-index-luke.com/|guide

=== TOPIC ===
id: backend-authentication-jwt-oauth
title: Authentication, Session Management, JWT & OAuth 2.0
roadmapId: backend-developer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 75
summary: Password hashing (Argon2, bcrypt), stateful sessions vs stateless JWTs, refresh tokens, and OAuth 2.0 / OIDC.
explanation: Authentication verifies user identity, while authorization controls access rights. Passwords must be salted and hashed with adaptive algorithms like Argon2id or bcrypt. Stateless authentication utilizes signed JSON Web Tokens (JWT) with header, payload, and HMAC/RSA signature.
codeExample: # Conceptual JWT Header + Payload + Signature\nimport hmac, hashlib, base64\nheader = base64.urlsafe_b64encode(b'{"alg":"HS256","typ":"JWT"}').rstrip(b'=')\npayload = base64.urlsafe_b64encode(b'{"sub":"user123","role":"admin"}').rstrip(b'=')\nsig = base64.urlsafe_b64encode(hmac.new(b'secret_key', header + b'.' + payload, hashlib.sha256).digest()).rstrip(b'=')\njwt = header + b'.' + payload + b'.' + sig\nprint("Generated JWT:", jwt.decode('utf-8'))
objective: Hash and verify passwords with bcrypt/Argon2 and unique salts
objective: Generate and verify cryptographically signed JWTs with expiry limits
objective: Implement secure OAuth 2.0 Authorization Code Flow with PKCE
pitfall: Storing plain text or MD5/SHA1 hashed passwords
pitfall: Storing sensitive personal data inside unencrypted JWT payloads
pitfall: Failing to implement token revocation or blacklisting for stolen JWTs
prerequisites: backend-relational-databases-sql
resource: OWASP Authentication Cheat Sheet|https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authentication_Cheat_Sheet.html|reference

=== TOPIC ===
id: backend-caching-redis
title: High-Performance Caching Strategies with Redis
roadmapId: backend-developer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 60
summary: In-memory key-value caching, eviction policies (LRU/LFU), cache patterns (Cache-Aside, Write-Through), and TTL.
explanation: Redis provides in-memory, sub-millisecond data storage supporting strings, hashes, lists, sets, and sorted sets. Caching reduces database load and network latency. The Cache-Aside pattern queries cache first, falling back to database on miss, and populating cache with a Time-To-Live (TTL).
codeExample: # Cache-Aside Pattern\ndef get_user(user_id):\n    # cached = redis.get(f"user:{user_id}")\n    # if cached: return json.loads(cached)\n    # db_user = query_db(user_id)\n    # redis.setex(f"user:{user_id}", 3600, json.dumps(db_user))\n    return f"User {user_id} fetched and cached"
objective: Implement Cache-Aside and Write-Through caching patterns with explicit TTLs
objective: Mitigate cache stampede (thundering herd) and cache penetration
objective: Select proper Redis memory eviction policies (allkeys-lru, volatile-ttl)
pitfall: Caching dynamic data indefinitely without TTL leading to stale state
pitfall: Cache stampede: simultaneous cache misses hitting database on hot key expiration
pitfall: Over-caching data with rapid write frequencies but low read rates
prerequisites: backend-authentication-jwt-oauth
resource: Redis Documentation: Caching Patterns|https://redis.io/docs/manual/client-side-caching/|documentation

