id: backend-automated-testing-cicd
title: Automated Testing, Unit/Integration & CI/CD Pipelines
roadmapId: backend-developer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 65
summary: Testing pyramid (Unit, Integration, E2E), test runners, mocking, and automated GitHub Actions workflows.
explanation: The testing pyramid emphasizes fast, comprehensive unit tests at the base, integration tests verifying database and API contracts in the middle, and end-to-end tests at the top. Continuous Integration (CI) runs test suites on every pull request, enforcing quality gates before deployment.
codeExample: # GitHub Actions Workflow snippet\n# name: CI\n# on: [push, pull_request]\n# jobs:\n#   test:\n#     runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n#     steps:\n#     - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n#     - run: gcc -O2 backend/*.c -o server && ./server --test\nprint("CI pipeline configured")
objective: Write comprehensive unit tests with assertions and test runners
objective: Construct integration tests with test containers or ephemeral databases
objective: Author automated CI/CD pipeline definitions (GitHub Actions / GitLab CI)
pitfall: Mocking out all database queries in integration tests, masking real SQL errors
pitfall: Writing flaky tests dependent on network availability or system clocks
pitfall: Low test coverage on edge cases and failure paths
prerequisites: backend-caching-redis
resource: Martin Fowler: The Practical Test Pyramid|https://martinfowler.com/articles/practical-test-pyramid.html|article

=== TOPIC ===
id: backend-docker-containerization
title: Containerization with Docker & Multi-Stage Builds
roadmapId: backend-developer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Advanced
estimatedMinutes: 75
summary: Containers vs VMs, Linux namespaces, cgroups, Dockerfile multi-stage builds, and Docker Compose.
explanation: Docker packages applications and runtime dependencies into immutable container images isolated by Linux namespaces (PID, NET, MNT) and cgroups. Multi-stage Dockerfiles separate the build environment (compilers, SDKs) from the final minimal production image (Alpine, distroless), slashing image size and attack surface.
codeExample: # Multi-Stage Dockerfile for C Backend\n# FROM gcc:15 AS builder\n# WORKDIR /app\n# COPY . .\n# RUN make\n# FROM alpine:latest\n# COPY --from=builder /app/server /usr/local/bin/server\n# CMD ["server"]
objective: Write multi-stage Dockerfiles producing minimal attack-surface production images
objective: Orchestrate multi-container backend stacks (App + Postgres + Redis) with Docker Compose
objective: Inspect container logs, resource usage, and network bridges
pitfall: Running containers as root user in production
pitfall: Including secrets, API keys, or build cache artifacts inside image layers
pitfall: Neglecting .dockerignore files resulting in bloated build contexts
prerequisites: backend-automated-testing-cicd
resource: Docker Best Practices for Developers|https://docs.docker.com/develop/develop-images/dockerfile_best-practices/|documentation

=== TOPIC ===
id: fullstack-web-architecture
title: Modern Full-Stack Web Architecture & Client-Server Lifecycle
roadmapId: fullstack-developer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Beginner
estimatedMinutes: 50
summary: Browser rendering pipeline, critical rendering path, client vs server side rendering (CSR vs SSR), and HTTP requests.
explanation: Full-stack web applications bridge client user interfaces with backend servers. When a URL is requested, browsers parse HTML to construct the DOM, evaluate CSS to build the CSSOM, compute render trees, layout geometry, and paint pixels. Client-Side Rendering (CSR) hydrates DOM via JavaScript, while Server-Side Rendering (SSR) serves pre-rendered HTML.
codeExample: <!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang="en">\n<head><title>FullStack App</title></head>\n<body>\n  <div id="app">Loading content...</div>\n  <script>\n    fetch('/api/stats')\n      .then(r => r.json())\n      .then(d => document.getElementById('app').textContent = `Roadmaps: ${d.roadmaps}`);\n  </script>\n</body>\n</html>
objective: Trace the browser Critical Rendering Path: HTML -> DOM -> CSSOM -> Layout -> Paint
objective: Evaluate architectural trade-offs between CSR, SSR, and Static Site Generation (SSG)
objective: Structure clean separation of concerns between client UI and backend APIs
pitfall: Render-blocking scripts delaying the First Contentful Paint (FCP)
pitfall: Tight coupling between UI display components and backend database schemas
prerequisites: 
resource: MDN Web Docs: How the Web Works|https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Learn/Common_questions/Web_mechanics/How_does_the_Internet_work|documentation

=== TOPIC ===
id: fullstack-dom-api
title: DOM Manipulation, Events & Asynchronous Fetch API
roadmapId: fullstack-developer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Beginner
estimatedMinutes: 60
summary: Document Object Model, event delegation, event bubbling/capturing, Promises, and async/await Fetch API.
explanation: The DOM provides an object-oriented representation of the web page. Event delegation captures events from child elements on common parent ancestors using event bubbling. Asynchronous network requests via the modern Fetch API and async/await retrieve backend JSON data without full page reloads.
codeExample: async function loadTopic(topicId) {\n  try {\n    const res = await fetch(`/api/topics/${topicId}`);\n    if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);\n    const data = await res.json();\n    console.log('Loaded:', data.title);\n  } catch (err) {\n    console.error('Fetch failed:', err);\n  }\n}
objective: Query, create, mutate, and remove DOM nodes cleanly and safely
objective: Implement high-performance event delegation listeners
objective: Execute robust async/await fetch requests with try/catch error handling
pitfall: Direct innerHTML assignments with untrusted strings causing Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)
pitfall: Registering individual event listeners on hundreds of cards rather than using event delegation
pitfall: Assuming fetch() rejects Promises on HTTP 4xx/5xx responses (it only rejects on network failure)
prerequisites: fullstack-web-architecture
resource: MDN Web Docs: Introduction to the DOM|https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Document_Object_Model/Introduction|documentation

=== TOPIC ===
id: fullstack-dsa-subroadmap
title: Data Structures & Algorithms Sub-Roadmap
roadmapId: fullstack-developer
type: subroadmap
targetSubroadmap: dsa
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 100
summary: Apply data structures to build rich interactive web apps: virtual DOM diffing, state trees, and graph roadmaps.
explanation: Full-stack engineers leverage data structures daily: tree traversals power DOM query selectors and component diffing, stacks power client-side history and undo buffers, queues coordinate async task loops, and graphs model prerequisites and routing networks.
codeExample: // Client-Side Navigation Stacks\nclass HistoryRouter {\n  constructor() {\n    this.backStack = [];\n    this.forwardStack = [];\n    this.current = null;\n  }\n  navigate(route) {\n    if (this.current) this.backStack.push(this.current);\n    this.current = route;\n    this.forwardStack = [];\n  }\n}
objective: Apply tree and graph algorithms to hierarchical UI component trees
objective: Model client-side navigation with stack data structures
objective: Analyze algorithmic efficiency in frontend filtering and sorting operations
pitfall: Executing O(N^2) filtering in frontend render loops causing UI thread freezing
pitfall: Memory leaks by retaining orphaned DOM nodes in client-side arrays
prerequisites: fullstack-dom-api
resource: PathForge Data Structures & Algorithms Track|#/roadmap/dsa|course

