resource: Full Stack Open: Communicating with Server|https://fullstackopen.com/en/part2/altering_data_in_server|course

=== TOPIC ===
id: fullstack-state-management
title: Client-Side State Management & Reactive UI Patterns
roadmapId: fullstack-developer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 70
summary: Single source of truth, unidirectional data flow, reactive state stores, and URL hash routing.
explanation: State represents all dynamic data driving user interfaces. Unidirectional data flow (Actions -> State -> View) prevents unpredictable cross-component mutations. Maintaining state synchronization between local memory, localStorage persistence, and backend databases ensures seamless user experience.
codeExample: class Store {\n  constructor(initialState) {\n    this.state = initialState;\n    this.listeners = [];\n  }\n  subscribe(fn) { this.listeners.push(fn); }\n  setState(patch) {\n    this.state = { ...this.state, ...patch };\n    this.listeners.forEach(fn => fn(this.state));\n  }\n}
objective: Architect a lightweight reactive state store with event subscription observers
objective: Sync application view state with URL hash routes to enable shareable links and browser back/forward
objective: Persist client preferences (theme, filters) across browser refreshes with localStorage
pitfall: Storing duplicated derived state that goes out of sync with raw state
pitfall: Mutating state objects directly rather than creating fresh copies
pitfall: Desynchronization between URL route parameters and visible UI state
prerequisites: fullstack-backend-integration
resource: Redux Core Principles & Unidirectional Flow|https://redux.js.org/understanding/thinking-in-redux/three-principles|documentation

=== TOPIC ===
id: fullstack-api-security-cors
title: Web Security: CORS, XSS, CSRF & Content Security Policy
roadmapId: fullstack-developer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 65
summary: Cross-Origin Resource Sharing, XSS sanitization, CSRF tokens, SameSite cookies, and CSP headers.
explanation: Web security defends against common application attack vectors. Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS) enforces browser same-origin policies on cross-domain API calls. Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) is mitigated through strict output encoding and Content Security Policy (CSP). Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF) is defended via anti-CSRF tokens and SameSite=Strict cookies.
codeExample: // Backend CORS & Security Headers\n// HTTP/1.1 200 OK\n// Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://pathforge.dev\n// Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self';\n// Set-Cookie: session=xyz; HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Strict\nconsole.log("Security headers active");
objective: Configure backend CORS headers (Access-Control-Allow-Origin/Methods) securely
objective: Sanitize untrusted user input to neutralize XSS injection vectors
objective: Secure session cookies using HttpOnly, Secure, and SameSite flags
pitfall: Setting Access-Control-Allow-Origin: * while allowing authenticated cookies
pitfall: Relying on client-side regexes for security sanitization
pitfall: Injecting raw HTML via innerHTML without DOMPurify sanitization
prerequisites: fullstack-state-management
resource: OWASP Top 10 Web Application Security Risks|https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/|reference

