id: c-linked-structures
title: Linked Lists, Stacks & Queues in C
roadmapId: c
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 75
summary: Implement pointer-linked nodes and common linear abstract data types.
explanation: A linked list stores nodes connected through pointers, while a stack follows last-in-first-out order and a queue follows first-in-first-out order. Each operation must preserve head, tail, and size invariants, handle empty structures, and release allocated nodes. These structures are useful for representing prerequisites, navigation history, and work queues.
codeExample: typedef struct Node {\n    int value;\n    struct Node *next;\n} Node;
objective: Implement insertion, traversal, and deletion for a linked list
objective: Explain stack LIFO and queue FIFO behavior
objective: Handle empty and single-node cases without leaks
pitfall: Losing the head pointer during insertion
pitfall: Dereferencing a null pointer on empty structures
pitfall: Freeing a node before saving its next pointer
prerequisites: c-pointers-memory,c-structs,c-dynamic-allocation
resource: Open Data Structures|https://opendatastructures.org/|textbook
resource: SEI CERT C|https://wiki.sei.cmu.edu/confluence/display/c|reference

