id: java-jdbc-database
title: JDBC & Database Access
roadmapId: java
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 60
summary: Connect Java applications to relational databases using parameterized SQL.
explanation: JDBC standardizes database connections, prepared statements, result sets, and transaction boundaries. PreparedStatement binds values separately from SQL text, reducing injection risk. Production code should close resources reliably and decide explicitly where transactions begin, commit, or roll back.
codeExample: try (PreparedStatement ps = conn.prepareStatement(\n        "SELECT name FROM users WHERE id = ?")) {\n    ps.setInt(1, userId);\n    try (ResultSet rs = ps.executeQuery()) {\n        while (rs.next()) System.out.println(rs.getString("name"));\n    }\n}
objective: Execute parameterized queries with PreparedStatement
objective: Read typed values from ResultSet
objective: Explain commit and rollback behavior
pitfall: Concatenating untrusted input into SQL
pitfall: Leaking connections or result sets
pitfall: Assuming auto-commit matches application transaction needs
prerequisites: java-classes-oop,java-exception-handling
resource: JDBC Basics|https://docs.oracle.com/javase/tutorial/jdbc/basics/|documentation
resource: Java SQL API|https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/21/docs/api/java.sql/module-summary.html|documentation

