id: backend-transactions-indexing
title: SQL Transactions, Indexes & Query Plans
roadmapId: backend-developer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 80
summary: Use transaction boundaries, indexes, constraints, and query plans to preserve correctness and performance.
explanation: Transactions group database changes into a unit with defined commit and rollback behavior. Indexes can accelerate reads but add storage and write costs. Query plans reveal whether the database scans, joins, or uses indexes, so optimization should follow measurement rather than guesswork.
codeExample: BEGIN;\nUPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = 1;\nUPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 100 WHERE id = 2;\nCOMMIT;
objective: Explain atomicity and isolation in transactions
objective: Choose indexes based on query patterns
objective: Read a query plan and identify expensive operations
pitfall: Assuming an index always improves performance
pitfall: Updating related records without a transaction
pitfall: Ignoring lock contention and isolation anomalies
prerequisites: backend-relational-databases-sql
resource: PostgreSQL Transactions|https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial-transactions.html|documentation
resource: PostgreSQL EXPLAIN|https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/using-explain.html|documentation

