=== TOPIC ===
id: dsa-complexity-arrays
title: Complexity, Arrays & Dynamic Sequences
roadmapId: dsa
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Core
estimatedMinutes: 70
summary: Analyze time and space costs and compare contiguous arrays with dynamic sequences.
explanation: Asymptotic complexity describes how resource use grows with input size. Arrays provide constant-time indexed access but insertion in the middle requires shifting elements. Dynamic arrays amortize append costs by occasionally allocating a larger backing store.
codeExample: // Typical costs: indexed access O(1), linear search O(n), append amortized O(1)
objective: Classify common operations with Big-O notation
objective: Compare arrays and linked lists by access and update patterns
objective: Explain amortized append cost
pitfall: Treating Big-O as an exact runtime measurement
pitfall: Ignoring memory overhead and cache locality
pitfall: Assuming linked lists make every operation faster
prerequisites: 
resource: Open Data Structures|https://opendatastructures.org/|textbook
resource: Big-O Cheat Sheet|https://www.bigocheatsheet.com/|reference

=== TOPIC ===
id: dsa-linked-lists-stacks-queues
title: Linked Lists, Stacks & Queues
roadmapId: dsa
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Core
estimatedMinutes: 80
summary: Model pointer-linked sequences and implement LIFO and FIFO structures.
explanation: Linked lists connect nodes through references, stacks remove the most recently added item, and queues remove the earliest item. These abstract data types support navigation history, expression evaluation, breadth-first search, and scheduling.
codeExample: // Stack: push/pop (LIFO); Queue: enqueue/dequeue (FIFO)
objective: Trace linked-list insertion and deletion
objective: Select a stack or queue based on required ordering
objective: State and preserve empty/non-empty invariants
pitfall: Losing references during pointer updates
pitfall: Confusing stack order with queue order
pitfall: Failing to handle underflow
prerequisites: dsa-complexity-arrays
resource: Open Data Structures: Lists|https://opendatastructures.org/|textbook
resource: VisuAlgo|https://visualgo.net/en/list|visualization

=== TOPIC ===
id: dsa-trees-hashing
title: Trees, Heaps & Hash Tables
roadmapId: dsa
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Core
estimatedMinutes: 90
summary: Use hierarchical and key-indexed structures to organize and retrieve data.
explanation: Trees represent hierarchy and support search patterns; heaps efficiently expose a minimum or maximum priority; hash tables map keys to buckets using a hash function. Performance depends on balance, collision handling, and workload assumptions.
codeExample: // Common uses: BST for ordered search, heap for priority queues, hash table for key lookup
objective: Compare tree traversal orders
objective: Explain heap invariants and priority queues
objective: Describe collisions and load factor in hash tables
pitfall: Assuming an unbalanced BST is always O(log n)
pitfall: Confusing heap order with full sorting
pitfall: Ignoring hash collisions
prerequisites: dsa-linked-lists-stacks-queues
resource: Open Data Structures|https://opendatastructures.org/|textbook
resource: VisuAlgo|https://visualgo.net/en|visualization

=== TOPIC ===
id: dsa-graphs-traversal
title: Graphs, BFS & DFS
roadmapId: dsa
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Core
estimatedMinutes: 90
summary: Represent relationships and traverse connected nodes with breadth-first and depth-first search.
explanation: Graphs model entities and relationships. Adjacency lists are often efficient for sparse graphs, while adjacency matrices provide constant-time edge lookup at quadratic space cost. BFS explores by distance layers; DFS explores paths and supports cycle detection and topological techniques.
codeExample: // BFS uses a queue; DFS uses recursion or an explicit stack.
objective: Choose adjacency lists or matrices based on graph density
objective: Trace BFS using a queue and DFS using a stack
objective: Identify when traversal needs a visited set
pitfall: Revisiting nodes in cyclic graphs
pitfall: Using DFS when shortest unweighted paths are required
pitfall: Assuming every graph is connected
prerequisites: dsa-trees-hashing
resource: CP-Algorithms Graphs|https://cp-algorithms.com/graph/breadth-first-search.html|reference
resource: VisuAlgo Graph Traversal|https://visualgo.net/en/dfsbfs|visualization

=== TOPIC ===
id: ml-model-selection
title: Model Selection & Cross-Validation
roadmapId: machine-learning
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Specialized
estimatedMinutes: 80
summary: Compare candidate models using disciplined validation and leakage-aware splits.
explanation: Cross-validation estimates generalization by repeating training and evaluation across folds. Split strategy must match the data-generating process: grouped observations may require group splits, while time series require chronological evaluation. Hyperparameter tuning should use training/validation data without repeatedly optimizing against the final test set.
codeExample: from sklearn.model_selection import cross_val_score, StratifiedKFold\ncv = StratifiedKFold(n_splits=5, shuffle=True, random_state=42)\nscores = cross_val_score(model, X, y, cv=cv, scoring="f1")
objective: Select a validation strategy aligned with data structure
objective: Separate tuning from final test evaluation
objective: Report variability as well as mean metrics
pitfall: Randomly splitting time-dependent observations
pitfall: Repeatedly tuning against the test set
pitfall: Using the wrong metric for class imbalance
prerequisites: aiml-model-evaluation-metrics
resource: Scikit-learn Cross-validation|https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/cross_validation.html|documentation
resource: Google ML: Data Splits|https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/overfitting/dividing-datasets|course

=== TOPIC ===
id: ml-feature-pipelines
title: Reproducible Feature Pipelines
roadmapId: machine-learning
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Specialized
estimatedMinutes: 65
summary: Package preprocessing and model transformations into a consistent training/inference pipeline.
explanation: A pipeline applies the same ordered transformations during training and prediction. It reduces accidental mismatch between notebook code and production inference and allows transformations to be cross-validated as part of the estimator.
codeExample: from sklearn.pipeline import Pipeline\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\npipe = Pipeline([("scale", StandardScaler()), ("model", LogisticRegression())])
objective: Chain transformations and estimators in a pipeline
objective: Fit preprocessing only on the training partition
objective: Persist pipeline metadata alongside the model
pitfall: Duplicating preprocessing logic in production
pitfall: Fitting transformations before cross-validation
pitfall: Changing feature order between training and inference
prerequisites: ml-model-selection
resource: Scikit-learn Pipelines|https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/compose.html|documentation
resource: MLflow Models|https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/ml/model/|documentation

=== TOPIC ===
id: backend-cache-design
title: Cache Design & Invalidation
roadmapId: backend-development
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Specialized
estimatedMinutes: 70
summary: Choose cache keys, TTLs, invalidation strategies, and consistency expectations.
explanation: Caches reduce repeated work but introduce stale-data and invalidation concerns. Cache-aside is a common pattern: read from cache, fall back to the source, and populate the cache. TTLs, versioned keys, and explicit invalidation should match the freshness requirements of each data type.
codeExample: key = f"user:{user_id}:profile:v1"\n# Cache-aside: read cache -> query database on miss -> set with TTL
objective: Compare cache-aside and write-through approaches
objective: Define key naming and expiration policy
objective: Explain stale reads and invalidation trade-offs
pitfall: Caching authorization decisions without careful invalidation
pitfall: Using unbounded cache keys
pitfall: Assuming cached data is always current
prerequisites: backend-transactions-indexing
resource: Redis Data Structures|https://redis.io/docs/latest/develop/data-types/|documentation
resource: AWS Caching Best Practices|https://aws.amazon.com/caching/best-practices/|guide

