id: backend-authz-secrets
title: Authorization, Secrets & Threat Modeling
roadmapId: backend-development
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Specialized
estimatedMinutes: 75
summary: Enforce least privilege, protect credentials, and reason about application threats.
explanation: Authentication establishes identity; authorization decides what that identity may do. Server-side checks must enforce ownership and permissions on every protected operation. Secrets should be injected through controlled configuration, rotated when exposed, and excluded from logs and source control.
codeExample: # Pseudocode: authorize the requested action against the authenticated principal\nif not policy.can_edit(user, resource):\n    raise PermissionError("forbidden")
objective: Distinguish authentication from authorization
objective: Apply least privilege and resource-level checks
objective: Store and rotate secrets outside source code
pitfall: Checking only whether a user is logged in
pitfall: Trusting client-supplied role values
pitfall: Committing API keys to version control
prerequisites: backend-api-versioning
resource: OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet|https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authorization_Cheat_Sheet.html|reference
resource: OWASP Secrets Management Cheat Sheet|https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Secrets_Management_Cheat_Sheet.html|reference

