summary: Apply least privilege, multifactor authentication, authorization checks, and credential lifecycle practices.
explanation: Authentication establishes an identity claim; authorization decides what that identity may do. Access should be scoped to the minimum permissions required and reviewed over time.
codeExample: 
objective: Differentiate authentication and authorization
objective: apply least privilege
objective: explain MFA and session expiry.
pitfall: Relying on client-side checks alone
pitfall: sharing service accounts
pitfall: storing plaintext passwords.
prerequisites: cyber-network-security
resource: OWASP Authentication Cheat Sheet|https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authentication_Cheat_Sheet.html|guide

=== TOPIC ===
id: cyber-web-vulnerabilities
title: Common Web Application Vulnerabilities
roadmapId: cybersecurity-analyst
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 90
summary: Recognize injection, broken access control, cross-site scripting, insecure design, and security misconfiguration.
explanation: Application weaknesses often occur where untrusted input crosses a trust boundary or where authorization is incomplete. Testing must be performed only on systems you own or have explicit permission to assess.
codeExample: 
objective: Map common risks to mitigations
objective: validate input and output encoding
objective: test authorization boundaries in a lab.
pitfall: Testing third-party systems without permission
pitfall: relying only on input filters for injection defense.
prerequisites: cyber-identity-access
resource: OWASP Top 10|https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/|standard

=== TOPIC ===
id: cyber-vulnerability-management
title: Vulnerability Assessment and Remediation
roadmapId: cybersecurity-analyst
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 90
summary: Inventory assets, prioritize findings, validate exposure, and track fixes through verification.
explanation: A vulnerability finding needs context: affected asset, exploitability, business impact, and compensating controls. Remediation is complete only after a fix or accepted mitigation is verified.
codeExample: 
objective: Prioritize findings using risk context
objective: distinguish scanning from validation
objective: document remediation evidence.
pitfall: Treating scanner severity as the full risk assessment
pitfall: closing findings without retesting.
prerequisites: cyber-web-vulnerabilities
resource: NIST National Vulnerability Database|https://nvd.nist.gov/|database

=== TOPIC ===
id: cyber-secure-development
title: Secure Development Lifecycle
roadmapId: cybersecurity-analyst
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 75
summary: Integrate security requirements, code review, dependency checks, and secrets handling into delivery.
explanation: Security practices are most effective when integrated throughout design, implementation, testing, and release. Dependency and secret scanning support review but require triage and remediation ownership.
codeExample: 
objective: Add security checks to CI
objective: review dependencies
objective: keep secrets out of source control.
pitfall: Assuming tools catch every flaw
pitfall: ignoring dependency advisories
pitfall: leaking secrets in logs.
prerequisites: cyber-vulnerability-management
resource: OWASP Cheat Sheet Series|https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/|guide

=== TOPIC ===
id: cyber-logging-siem
title: Security Logging and Detection
roadmapId: cybersecurity-analyst
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Advanced
estimatedMinutes: 90
summary: Collect useful audit events and investigate suspicious patterns across hosts, identities, and applications.
explanation: Detection depends on reliable event context, time synchronization, retention, and alert logic. A SIEM can correlate events but cannot compensate for missing telemetry or poorly defined use cases.
codeExample: 
objective: Identify useful authentication and system events
objective: correlate related events
objective: write a detection hypothesis.
pitfall: Logging sensitive data unnecessarily
pitfall: alerting without investigation procedures.
prerequisites: cyber-secure-development
resource: MITRE ATT&CK|https://attack.mitre.org/|knowledge base

