=== TOPIC ===
id: db-sql-querying
title: SQL Querying and Joins
roadmapId: database-engineer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Beginner
estimatedMinutes: 90
summary: Query, filter, aggregate, and combine relational data using joins and grouping.
explanation: SQL expresses set-oriented operations. Joins combine rows according to predicates, while aggregation summarizes groups; query results should be interpreted with null and duplicate behavior in mind.
codeExample: 
objective: Write SELECT queries with joins and aggregates
objective: reason about NULL
objective: use grouping and subqueries.
pitfall: Accidentally multiplying rows through many-to-many joins
pitfall: using ambiguous column names.
prerequisites: db-modeling-normalization
resource: PostgreSQL Tutorial|https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html|documentation

=== TOPIC ===
id: db-transactions
title: Transactions and Isolation
roadmapId: database-engineer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 90
summary: Use transactions and understand atomicity, consistency, isolation, durability, and concurrency anomalies.
explanation: Transactions group operations into a unit of work. Isolation levels provide different guarantees and performance trade-offs; applications must handle conflicts and retries where appropriate.
codeExample: 
objective: Explain ACID
objective: choose transaction boundaries
objective: recognize dirty reads and serialization conflicts.
pitfall: Holding transactions open too long
pitfall: assuming every database has identical isolation behavior.
prerequisites: db-sql-querying
resource: PostgreSQL Transaction Isolation|https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/transaction-iso.html|documentation

=== TOPIC ===
id: db-indexes-query-plans
title: Indexes and Query Plans
roadmapId: database-engineer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 90
summary: Use indexes and query plans to understand access paths and improve expensive queries.
explanation: Indexes can reduce lookup cost but add storage and write overhead. Query planners estimate plan costs, so optimization should be guided by representative data and measurements.
codeExample: 
objective: Read EXPLAIN output
objective: choose indexes for observed query patterns
objective: compare plans before and after changes.
pitfall: Indexing every column
pitfall: tuning against tiny test data only
pitfall: ignoring write overhead.
prerequisites: db-transactions
resource: PostgreSQL EXPLAIN|https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/using-explain.html|documentation

=== TOPIC ===
id: db-migrations
title: Schema Migrations and Change Management
roadmapId: database-engineer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 75
summary: Evolve schemas with versioned migrations, safe rollout strategies, and recovery plans.
explanation: Migrations make schema changes reproducible across environments. Production changes may require backward-compatible expand-and-contract steps when old and new application versions run simultaneously.
codeExample: 
objective: Write ordered migrations
objective: plan rollback or forward-fix strategies
objective: keep deployment compatibility in mind.
pitfall: Editing production schemas manually
pitfall: deploying incompatible schema and application changes together.
prerequisites: db-indexes-query-plans
resource: Django Migrations Documentation|https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/stable/topics/migrations/|documentation

=== TOPIC ===
id: db-backup-recovery
title: Backup, Restore and Recovery
roadmapId: database-engineer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Advanced
estimatedMinutes: 90
summary: Create backups and verify restoration against recovery point and recovery time objectives.
explanation: A backup is useful only if it can be restored within acceptable time and data-loss limits. Recovery planning includes retention, access control, consistency, and periodic restore tests.
codeExample: 
objective: Explain RPO and RTO
objective: document backup schedules
objective: test a restore into an isolated environment.
pitfall: Assuming a successful backup job proves recoverability
pitfall: storing all backups under the same compromised credentials.
prerequisites: db-migrations
resource: PostgreSQL Backup and Restore|https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/backup.html|documentation

