=== TOPIC ===
id: mleng-data-preparation
title: Data Preparation and Feature Engineering
roadmapId: machine-learning-engineer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 70
summary: Create reliable training data and features without leaking information from evaluation data.
explanation: Inspect missingness, label quality, class imbalance, data types, and sampling. Split data before fitting preprocessing steps; use pipelines so transformations are learned only from training folds. Engineer features from information available at prediction time.
codeExample: from sklearn.pipeline import make_pipeline
from sklearn.impute import SimpleImputer
from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler
from sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression

model = make_pipeline(SimpleImputer(), StandardScaler(), LogisticRegression())
model.fit(X_train, y_train)  # fit preprocessing only on training data
objective: Build a reproducible preprocessing pipeline
objective: Select an appropriate split strategy
objective: Explain leakage from future or target-derived features
pitfall: Fitting scalers or imputers before splitting
pitfall: Randomly splitting time-dependent or grouped records
prerequisites: mleng-problem-framing
resource: Scikit-learn: Preprocessing data|https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/preprocessing.html|documentation
resource: Scikit-learn: Common pitfalls|https://scikit-learn.org/stable/common_pitfalls.html|documentation

=== TOPIC ===
id: mleng-model-training
title: Model Training and Regularization
roadmapId: machine-learning-engineer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 80
summary: Train candidate models while controlling overfitting and tracking reproducible experiments.
explanation: Establish a simple baseline, then compare suitable models using a consistent validation procedure. Regularization, early stopping, cross-validation, and hyperparameter search help manage generalization. Save seeds, data versions, parameters, metrics, and artifacts needed to reproduce results.
codeExample: 
objective: Compare a baseline against a candidate model
objective: Use cross-validation or a valid holdout strategy
objective: Record experiment configuration and results
pitfall: Tuning repeatedly on the final test set
pitfall: Comparing models on different data splits
prerequisites: mleng-data-preparation
resource: Scikit-learn: Model selection|https://scikit-learn.org/stable/model_selection.html|documentation
resource: PyTorch tutorials|https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/|documentation

=== TOPIC ===
id: mleng-evaluation
title: Model Evaluation and Error Analysis
roadmapId: machine-learning-engineer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 65
summary: Evaluate model quality, calibration, subgroup performance, and failure modes.
explanation: Report metrics with a confusion matrix or task-specific error analysis. For imbalanced classes, accuracy alone can be misleading. Consider calibration, confidence intervals, subgroup behavior, and the operational cost of false positives and false negatives.
codeExample: from sklearn.metrics import classification_report

y_pred = model.predict(X_test)
print(classification_report(y_test, y_pred))
objective: Choose metrics that match error costs
objective: Inspect representative false predictions
objective: Evaluate relevant data slices and uncertainty
pitfall: Reporting only a single aggregate score
pitfall: Treating validation performance as a guarantee of production performance
prerequisites: mleng-model-training
resource: Scikit-learn: Model evaluation|https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/model_evaluation.html|documentation

=== TOPIC ===
id: mleng-serving
title: Model Serving and Inference Contracts
roadmapId: machine-learning-engineer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Advanced
estimatedMinutes: 65
summary: Package a trained model behind a stable and testable inference interface.
explanation: A serving contract specifies input schema, output schema, preprocessing, model version, error handling, and latency expectations. Batch inference and online inference have different throughput and freshness requirements. Validate inputs and version the model with its preprocessing assets.
codeExample: from fastapi import FastAPI
from pydantic import BaseModel

app = FastAPI()
class Features(BaseModel):
    values: list[float]

@app.post("/predict")
def predict(data: Features):
    return {"prediction": model.predict([data.values]).tolist()}
objective: Define and validate an inference schema
objective: Distinguish batch from online serving
objective: Measure latency and throughput
pitfall: Training and serving preprocessing diverging
pitfall: Deploying a model without a rollback path
prerequisites: mleng-evaluation
resource: MLflow: Model deployment|https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/ml/deployment/|documentation
resource: FastAPI documentation|https://fastapi.tiangolo.com/|documentation

=== TOPIC ===
id: mleng-monitoring
title: Production Model Monitoring
roadmapId: machine-learning-engineer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Advanced
estimatedMinutes: 55
summary: Monitor technical health and data/model behavior after deployment.
explanation: Track service latency, error rates, resource use, input distributions, delayed ground-truth metrics, and changes in important subgroups. A drift alert is a signal to investigate, not proof that model quality has declined; retraining should follow a validated process.
codeExample: 
objective: Separate service metrics from model-quality metrics
objective: Define alert thresholds and ownership
objective: Design a safe review and retraining trigger
pitfall: Retraining automatically on every drift alert
pitfall: Monitoring inputs without checking eventual outcomes
prerequisites: mleng-serving
resource: Evidently AI documentation|https://docs.evidentlyai.com/|documentation
resource: Google: Production ML systems|https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/production-ml-systems|guide

