id: net-transport-troubleshooting
title: TCP, UDP, TLS, and Network Troubleshooting
roadmapId: network-engineer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 70
summary: Use transport and application evidence to narrow connectivity failures.
explanation: TCP provides ordered reliable byte-stream delivery; UDP provides datagrams without built-in delivery guarantees. TLS protects a connection when correctly configured. Troubleshooting should distinguish DNS, routing, port reachability, handshake, certificate, and application-layer failures.
codeExample: curl -v --connect-timeout 5 https://example.com/
nslookup example.com

# Use packet capture only on networks and traffic you are authorized to inspect.
objective: Compare TCP and UDP use cases
objective: Interpret basic connection and TLS errors
objective: Use packet capture or socket tools carefully
pitfall: Assuming a port being open proves the application is healthy
pitfall: Disabling certificate checks as a permanent fix
prerequisites: net-dns-dhcp
resource: RFC Editor: TCP specification|https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9293|standard
resource: Wireshark documentation|https://www.wireshark.org/docs/|documentation

=== TOPIC ===
id: net-firewalls-segmentation
title: Firewalls, Segmentation, and Zero Trust
roadmapId: network-engineer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 60
summary: Reduce network exposure through explicit traffic policy and segmented trust boundaries.
explanation: Firewalls enforce traffic rules; segmentation limits lateral movement and scopes failure. A zero-trust approach verifies access based on identity and context rather than assuming that network location alone establishes trust. Document intended flows and test both allowed and denied paths.
codeExample: 
objective: Write least-privilege traffic rules
objective: Separate public, application, and data tiers
objective: Test denied as well as permitted flows
pitfall: Using broad any-any rules to resolve a connectivity issue
pitfall: Treating an internal network as inherently trusted
prerequisites: net-transport-troubleshooting
resource: NIST SP 800-207 Zero Trust Architecture|https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/207/final|standard
resource: CISA Zero Trust Maturity Model|https://www.cisa.gov/zero-trust-maturity-model|framework

