id: sysprog-process-memory
title: Processes, Virtual Memory, and Runtime
roadmapId: systems-programmer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 65
summary: Understand process isolation, virtual address spaces, and program execution at the OS boundary.
explanation: A process owns resources and a virtual address space; threads within a process share much of that state. Virtual memory separates logical addresses from physical memory and supports isolation, mapping, and demand paging. Exact APIs differ across operating systems.
codeExample: 
objective: Distinguish process and thread resources
objective: Explain virtual memory and page faults
objective: Inspect process state with OS tools
pitfall: Assuming virtual addresses are physical addresses
pitfall: Sharing mutable process state without synchronization
prerequisites: 
resource: Linux man-pages project|https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/|reference
resource: OSTEP: Operating Systems Three Easy Pieces|https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/|book

=== TOPIC ===
id: sysprog-pointers-ownership
title: Memory Ownership and Resource Lifetimes
roadmapId: systems-programmer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 65
summary: Manage memory and other resources with explicit ownership and lifetime rules.
explanation: In C, the programmer must define who allocates, owns, transfers, and frees each resource. Dangling references, double frees, leaks, and use-after-free errors arise when lifetime rules are unclear. Tools such as sanitizers can expose many memory errors during testing.
codeExample: #include <stdlib.h>

int *make_value(int value) {
    int *p = malloc(sizeof *p);
    if (p == NULL) return NULL;
    *p = value;
    return p; /* caller owns the allocation and must free it */
}
objective: Document ownership for API parameters and returns
objective: Handle allocation failure and cleanup paths
objective: Use sanitizers to find memory errors
pitfall: Freeing the same allocation twice
pitfall: Returning pointers whose storage lifetime has ended
prerequisites: sysprog-process-memory
resource: SEI CERT C Coding Standard|https://wiki.sei.cmu.edu/confluence/display/c|reference
resource: AddressSanitizer documentation|https://clang.llvm.org/docs/AddressSanitizer.html|documentation

=== TOPIC ===
id: sysprog-files-syscalls
title: System Calls, Files, and I/O
roadmapId: systems-programmer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 60
summary: Use operating-system interfaces for file descriptors, processes, and I/O safely.
explanation: System calls cross the user/kernel boundary to request OS services. Low-level I/O can return partial results or be interrupted; robust code checks return values and handles errors. File descriptors and handles have lifetimes and permissions.
codeExample: #include <unistd.h>
#include <errno.h>

ssize_t n;
do {
    n = write(fd, buffer, length);
} while (n < 0 && errno == EINTR);
/* Production code must also handle partial writes and other errors. */
objective: Check partial reads and writes
objective: Handle errors and resource cleanup
objective: Distinguish buffered library I/O from system calls
pitfall: Assuming one read always returns all requested bytes
pitfall: Ignoring permissions and path-handling risks
prerequisites: sysprog-pointers-ownership
resource: Linux man-pages: system calls|https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/syscalls.2.html|reference
resource: The Open Group POSIX specifications|https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9799919799/|standard

=== TOPIC ===
id: sysprog-concurrency
title: Threads, Synchronization, and Concurrency
roadmapId: systems-programmer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Advanced
estimatedMinutes: 75
summary: Coordinate concurrent execution without data races or deadlocks.
explanation: Threads can improve responsiveness and throughput but introduce shared-state hazards. Mutexes, condition variables, atomics, and message passing have different semantics. Correctness depends on invariants, lock ordering, and ownership, not simply on adding locks.
codeExample: /* Shared state must be protected by a consistent synchronization rule. */
pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);
shared_count += 1;
pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
objective: Identify shared mutable state
objective: Use synchronization primitives appropriately
objective: Explain race conditions and deadlock conditions
pitfall: Holding locks across unpredictable blocking operations
pitfall: Assuming atomicity of compound operations
prerequisites: sysprog-files-syscalls
resource: Linux man-pages: pthreads|https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/pthreads.7.html|reference
resource: C++ Core Guidelines: concurrency|https://isocpp.github.io/CppCoreGuidelines/CppCoreGuidelines#S-concurrency|guide

=== TOPIC ===
id: sysprog-debugging
title: Native Debugging and Performance Profiling
roadmapId: systems-programmer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Advanced
estimatedMinutes: 60
summary: Diagnose native crashes and performance bottlenecks using symbols, traces, and profilers.
explanation: A reproducible failure and a minimal test case are often more valuable than speculative fixes. Debuggers inspect execution state; profilers reveal where time or resources are spent. Benchmarks must reflect realistic workloads and account for measurement noise.
codeExample: 
objective: Capture a useful stack trace
objective: Use sanitizers and a profiler to test hypotheses
objective: Benchmark before and after a change
pitfall: Optimizing based on intuition alone
pitfall: Removing debug checks without identifying the root cause
prerequisites: sysprog-concurrency
resource: GDB documentation|https://sourceware.org/gdb/documentation/|documentation
resource: Linux perf documentation|https://perf.wiki.kernel.org/index.php/Main_Page|documentation

