id: emb-peripherals-buses
title: GPIO, Timers, UART, SPI, and I²C
roadmapId: embedded-systems-engineer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 70
summary: Interface firmware with external components using common peripheral and serial-bus patterns.
explanation: GPIO handles digital signals; timers support periodic events and measurement; UART, SPI, and I²C provide different serial communication trade-offs. Correct integration requires matching voltage levels, clocking, bus addresses, timing, and device-specific protocol details.
codeExample: /* Pseudocode: always check the HAL return code and use a timeout. */
status = i2c_read(device_address, register_address, buffer, length, timeout_ms);
if (status != OK) {
    record_bus_error(status);
}
objective: Choose an interface based on device requirements
objective: Read a peripheral timing diagram
objective: Handle communication errors and timeouts
pitfall: Assuming buses share the same electrical characteristics
pitfall: Ignoring pull-ups, clock rates, or voltage compatibility
prerequisites: emb-c-firmware
resource: Zephyr device driver model|https://docs.zephyrproject.org/latest/kernel/drivers/index.html|documentation
resource: Linux kernel driver API|https://docs.kernel.org/driver-api/|documentation

