difficulty: Advanced
estimatedMinutes: 65
summary: Design experiments that distinguish meaningful effects from confounding and random variation.
explanation: Randomization can reduce confounding in treatment assignment; observational data often needs stronger assumptions and careful design for causal claims. Predefine outcomes, sample size reasoning, exclusions, and stopping rules where possible.
codeExample: 
objective: Define treatment, outcome, and comparison
objective: Identify confounders in observational data
objective: Avoid interpreting correlation as causation
prerequisites: ds-probability-inference
resource: The Effect: An Introduction to Research Design and Causality|https://theeffectbook.net/|textbook
resource: NIST/SEMATECH e-Handbook of Statistical Methods|https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/|reference

=== TOPIC ===
id: ds-exploration-features
title: Exploratory Analysis and Feature Construction
roadmapId: data-scientist
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 60
summary: Investigate distributions, missingness, anomalies, and relationships before modeling.
explanation: Exploratory analysis helps reveal data issues and generate hypotheses, but repeated inspection can influence model choices. Record decisions, compare segments, and build transformations into a reproducible pipeline that avoids target leakage.
codeExample: import pandas as pd

print(df.isna().mean().sort_values(ascending=False))
print(df.describe(include="all"))
print(df.duplicated().sum())
objective: Inspect distributions and missingness
objective: Check duplicates, outliers, and label quality
objective: Separate exploration from final evaluation
prerequisites: ds-experiment-design
resource: Pandas user guide|https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/user_guide/|documentation
resource: Seaborn tutorial|https://seaborn.pydata.org/tutorial.html|documentation

=== TOPIC ===
id: ds-model-selection
title: Model Selection and Validation
roadmapId: data-scientist
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Advanced
estimatedMinutes: 70
summary: Compare predictive approaches with a validation design appropriate to the data-generating process.
explanation: Use holdout, cross-validation, grouped, or time-based splits according to the setting. Feature selection and hyperparameter tuning belong inside the validation process; preserve a final test set for the final evaluation when feasible.
codeExample: 
objective: Choose a split matching deployment conditions
objective: Compare against a simple baseline
objective: Document uncertainty and subgroup performance
prerequisites: ds-exploration-features
resource: Scikit-learn model selection|https://scikit-learn.org/stable/model_selection.html|documentation

=== TOPIC ===
id: ds-uncertainty-communication
title: Uncertainty, Limitations, and Data Storytelling
roadmapId: data-scientist
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 50
summary: Communicate what the evidence supports, what remains uncertain, and what decisions follow.
explanation: A useful analysis states the population, time window, data quality, assumptions, uncertainty, and practical magnitude of findings. Charts should preserve context and avoid implying causal or precise conclusions that the data cannot support.
codeExample: 
objective: Summarize effect size and uncertainty
objective: State limitations and population scope
objective: Choose a chart that does not distort comparisons
prerequisites: ds-model-selection
resource: Storytelling with Data resources|https://www.storytellingwithdata.com/|learning resource
resource: NIST statistical methods handbook|https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/|reference

=== TOPIC ===
id: ds-reproducible-work
title: Reproducible Data Science Workflows
roadmapId: data-scientist
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 45
summary: Make analyses rerunnable through versioned code, documented environments, and explicit data provenance.
explanation: Notebooks are useful for exploration but should have a clear execution order and reproducible dependencies. Separate raw, cleaned, and derived data; record how data was obtained and transformed, and avoid embedding credentials or sensitive records in notebooks.
codeExample: 
objective: Rerun analysis from a clean environment
objective: Document data provenance and transformations
objective: Separate secrets from code and outputs
prerequisites: ds-uncertainty-communication
resource: Jupyter documentation|https://docs.jupyter.org/en/latest/|documentation
resource: DVC documentation|https://dvc.org/doc|documentation

